# Salesforce Lightning Design System 2 (SLDS2) & LWC Development Rules

## Core SLDS2 Principles
- ALWAYS use SLDS utility classes instead of custom CSS
- ALWAYS follow SLDS component patterns and markup structure  
- ALWAYS use semantic HTML with proper SLDS classes
- NEVER create custom CSS that conflicts with SLDS design tokens
- ALWAYS use SLDS spacing, typography, and color utilities

## Lightning Web Component (LWC) Standards

### HTML Structure
- Use `<div class="slds-*">` containers for all components
- Apply `slds-grid` and `slds-col` for responsive layouts
- Use `slds-form-element` for all form inputs
- Always include proper SLDS button variants: `slds-button slds-button_brand`
- Use `slds-card` structure: `slds-card__header`, `slds-card__body`, `slds-card__footer`

### Required SLDS Classes by Component Type
- **Buttons**: `slds-button slds-button_[variant]` (brand, neutral, destructive, etc.)
- **Forms**: `slds-form-element`, `slds-form-element__label`, `slds-form-element__control`
- **Cards**: `slds-card`, `slds-card__header`, `slds-card__body`
- **Modals**: `slds-modal`, `slds-modal__container`, `slds-modal__content`
- **Tables**: `slds-table slds-table_bordered slds-table_col-bordered`
- **Lists**: `slds-list_dotted`, `slds-list_ordered`, `slds-item`

### Spacing & Layout
- Use SLDS spacing utilities: `slds-m-*`, `slds-p-*` (none, xxx-small, xx-small, x-small, small, medium, large, x-large, xx-large)
- Use SLDS grid system: `slds-grid`, `slds-col`, `slds-size_*`
- Use SLDS flexbox utilities: `slds-grid slds-gutters`, `slds-col`

### Typography
- Use SLDS text utilities: `slds-text-heading_*`, `slds-text-body_*`
- Use SLDS font weights: `slds-text-font_monospace`, `slds-text-font_*`
- Use SLDS text colors: `slds-text-color_*` (default, weak, error, success, etc.)

### Icons & Images
- Always use SLDS icons with proper sizing: `slds-icon slds-icon_*`
- Use SLDS image utilities for responsive images: `slds-image`
- Follow SLDS icon guidelines for accessibility

## JavaScript Standards

### LWC Best Practices
- Always import LWC base classes: `import { LightningElement } from 'lwc';`
- Use proper lifecycle methods: `connectedCallback()`, `renderedCallback()`
- Always use `@track`, `@api`, `@wire` decorators appropriately
- Use proper event handling with `this.dispatchEvent()`

### Error Handling
- Always include try-catch blocks for Apex calls
- Use proper SLDS error messaging with `slds-notify slds-notify_alert`
- Display errors in SLDS format: `slds-form-element__help slds-text-color_error`

### Accessibility
- Always include proper ARIA labels and roles
- Use SLDS accessibility classes: `slds-assistive-text`
- Ensure proper focus management and keyboard navigation
- Use semantic HTML elements with SLDS classes

## Apex Integration
- Always use `@AuraEnabled(cacheable=true)` for read operations
- Use proper error handling with `AuraHandledException`
- Follow SOQL best practices with selective field queries
- Always include security checks: `WITH SECURITY_ENFORCED`

## File Structure Standards
- Place all LWC files in proper structure: `component.html`, `component.js`, `component.js-meta.xml`
- Use descriptive component names in camelCase
- Include proper component metadata with target configs

## CSS Guidelines (When Custom CSS is Needed)
- Only use CSS custom properties (CSS variables) aligned with SLDS design tokens
- Always scope custom CSS to component level
- Use SLDS custom property names: `--slds-c-*` for component properties
- Never override core SLDS classes directly

## Data Import & CSV Standards
- Always use proper field API names in CSV headers
- Include proper HTML formatting in rich text fields using SLDS classes
- Use proper image URL references for GitHub raw content
- Follow proper lookup field formatting for relationships

## Documentation Standards
- Always include comprehensive inline comments
- Use proper JSDoc format for all public methods
- Include component usage examples in comments
- Document all @api properties with descriptions

## Testing Requirements
- Include proper test coverage for all LWC components
- Test all SLDS responsive breakpoints
- Validate accessibility compliance
- Test with different Salesforce user profiles and permissions

## Never Do
- Never use inline styles instead of SLDS classes
- Never create custom CSS that duplicates SLDS functionality
- Never use non-semantic HTML elements
- Never ignore SLDS accessibility guidelines
- Never use hardcoded colors, fonts, or spacing values
- Never create components without proper SLDS structure

## Always Do
- Always validate SLDS markup structure
- Always use SLDS design tokens for consistency
- Always test components in different Salesforce editions
- Always follow SLDS responsive design patterns
- Always include proper error states and loading indicators using SLDS
- Always use SLDS animation and transition utilities 